[06:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Flash more than doubling the current Flash on some sections. But, and this matters, the chart literally says internal evaluation at the top. These numbers came from Sapien\'s testing their'

[07:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'own unreleased models. Nobody independent has touched them yet. Self-reported benchmarks usually look a little better than reality, sometimes a lot better. So, read them as this lab is'

[07:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'playing in the right league, not this model beats Claude. The real test happens when 2.5 actually ships and people run it on their own projects. What\'s harder to fake is the current usage. In one recent week, Agnes'

[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'models processed 5.41 trillion tokens combined. 3.25 trillion of that was text. The rest was image and video work. That\'s sustained production traffic, all'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'served for free. Their argument is that the constraint on agentic coding right now isn\'t capability, it\'s cost. Agents burn tokens fast because they loop, retry, and reread files constantly. A single'

[08:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'long agent session on a paid frontier model can cost several dollars, and that adds up quick across a work day. Free with no cap removes that math entirely. There\'s a timing angle, too.'

[08:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Over the past few months, a lot of developers, especially outside the US, got hit with account suspensions and regional blocks on tools like Codex. Some people lost their main coding setup overnight. Agnes is clearly aiming at'

[08:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that crowd, developers who got priced out or locked out. Whether that affects you or not, more competition at the free tier pressures every other labs pricing, and that helps everyone.'

